From 7cbffcb92ce5e8249399ae240fe37d625fd4e5b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nik Samokhvalov <nik@postgres.ai>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 18:53:19 -0800
Subject: [PATCH v3] Validate inherited CHECK constraints when enabling
 enforcement

ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT on a child can merge a local ENFORCED
CHECK constraint into an inherited NOT ENFORCED one.  The merge marked
the constraint enforced and validated, but the existing rows had never
been checked against it, so a table could end up with a valid
constraint that some of its rows violate.  ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID
was marked validated the same way.

MergeWithExistingConstraint() now sets convalidated from the new
constraint's initially_valid.  ATAddCheckNNConstraint() remembers when
the merge enables enforcement and then reuses the CHECK enforceability
traversal to queue the Phase 3 check on the named relation and to
propagate enforcement to its descendants, without changing their
inheritance counts.  The checks of normal ADD CONSTRAINT recursion are
kept: ONLY is rejected when there are children, and permissions are
checked on each descendant.

A descendant whose constraint is already ENFORCED but NOT VALID makes
the command fail with the error added by commit 0b12a5a7435; it must be
validated first.  With NOT VALID, nothing is marked validated, so such a
descendant is left as is and the command succeeds.

Author: Nik Samokhvalov <nik@postgres.ai>
Reviewed-by: Chao Li <li.evan.chao@gmail.com>
Backpatch-through: 18
Discussion: https://postgr.es/m/CAM527d8qCH4q1xV-TftDmm3eBcBie9AxfdTtThPUoGWnYT08gw@mail.gmail.com
